# Red Pitaya Resonance Tracker — Codex Working Guide

## Mission

Build a self-contained Red Pitaya web application for a two-sensor resonance
tracker. The application will use the FPGA raw coherent I/Q measurement already
added to this repository, run search/fitting/tracking on the ARM CPU, and expose
configuration, telemetry, and diagnostics in the browser.

Create the application at:

```text
apps-tools/resonance_tracker/
```

Use `apps-tools/impedance_analyzer/` as a reference for Red Pitaya application
lifecycle, build integration, parameter/signal transport, WebSocket use, and UI
patterns. Do not reuse its impedance-measurement algorithm or calibration model.

## Current project status

Milestone 1, the minimal raw-IQ web application, is implemented and working.
Treat it as the regression baseline. Inspect and validate the existing code before
changing it; do not recreate, replace, or substantially restructure the working
raw-IQ path merely to match the suggested file layout in this guide.

The active implementation scope is now Milestone 2A: introduce the application
state model and multi-dashboard frontend, then implement baseline acquisition,
resonance finding, and an RTM-like diagnostics mode. Milestone 2B will add
continuous tracking, quality monitoring, loss detection, and relocking. Preserve
all working Milestone 1 features throughout this work.

## Mandatory first step: inspect before editing

This guide does not know the exact names or layout of the locally added FPGA
raw-IQ registers. Do not guess them.

Before creating or modifying code:

1. Read the repository-level and nearest directory-level `AGENTS.md` files.
2. Inspect `git status`, the current branch, and the diff against its upstream or
   merge base. Preserve all existing user changes.
3. Locate the raw-IQ FPGA modifications and trace their complete data path:
   HDL/register definition -> address map -> low-level C API -> current caller or
   test code.
4. Inspect the actual `apps-tools/impedance_analyzer` implementation and the
   repository's current web-app examples. The checked-out source is authoritative;
   do not assume an API from older Red Pitaya documentation.
5. Write a short discovery note in
   `apps-tools/resonance_tracker/docs/raw_iq_interface.md` that records:
   - source files and relevant symbols;
   - register names, addresses/offsets, widths, signedness, scaling, and units;
   - accumulator/sample count and overflow behavior;
   - ready/busy/valid/sequence semantics;
   - reset, trigger, and frequency-change sequencing;
   - atomicity requirements when reading I and Q;
   - expected I/Q convention and phase/sign convention;
   - maximum safe update rate and known limitations;
   - any unanswered question marked explicitly as `TODO(user)`.

Useful discovery commands include `rg`, `git diff`, `git log -p`, and inspection
of generated register headers. Avoid broad generated/build directories.

Stop and ask the user if the raw-IQ interface cannot be identified unambiguously,
or if using it would require changing the FPGA register contract.

## Target architecture

Keep hardware access, tracking, transport, and presentation separate:

```text
Browser UI
  -> Red Pitaya parameter/signal transport
  -> thin web-app adapter
  -> tracker engine
  -> raw-IQ acquisition adapter
  -> existing FPGA register/API interface
```

Suggested source organization (adapt it to the conventions actually present in
the checked-out Red Pitaya tree):

```text
apps-tools/resonance_tracker/
  Makefile
  index.html
  info/
  css/
  js/
  src/
    main.cpp                 # Red Pitaya app lifecycle and thin adapter only
    raw_iq_acquisition.*     # FPGA/API access, scaling, coherent snapshots
    tracker_engine.*         # state machine and orchestration
    resonance_fit.*          # fitting/model code
    tracking_quality.*       # residuals, SE, loss/relock criteria
    telemetry_snapshot.*     # immutable UI-facing snapshot
  docs/
    raw_iq_interface.md
    architecture.md
```

Do not put DSP, fitting, or tracker state-machine logic in JavaScript. Do not put
HTML/WebSocket concerns in the tracker engine. `main.cpp` should remain a thin
Red Pitaya-specific adapter.

## Measurement constraints

- Hardware: Red Pitaya STEMlab 125-14 Gen 2 unless repository evidence says this
  branch targets another board.
- ADC/DAC clock: nominally 125 MS/s; obtain the effective clock and frequency
  tuning rules from the existing implementation rather than hard-coding
  assumptions.
- Primary operating region: approximately 30-34 MHz.
- The reflected signal is measured while the excitation/reference is coherent.
- The FPGA now provides raw coherent I and Q. Preserve raw integer values for
  diagnostics and convert to normalized/scaled values in one documented layer.
- The product goal is resonance variation/perturbation detection, not
  metrology-grade absolute impedance.
- Support two logical sensors, with each sensor independently hideable/disableable
  in the UI.
- Browser activity must not control or block measurement timing.

Do not change FPGA logic, register addresses, bit widths, or generated register
files as part of the initial web application unless the user explicitly approves
that scope.

## Timing and concurrency rules

Treat acquisition/tracking and browser telemetry as different rates:

- acquisition/DSP: fastest stable rate supported by the existing IQ interface;
- tracking estimator: driven by valid coherent measurements;
- web telemetry: initially 10-20 Hz, configurable later if justified;
- plots: decimate/bound history so the browser cannot create unbounded memory use.

Publish UI data through a coherent snapshot. A browser update must not observe a
partially updated pair of sensors or mismatched I/Q/sequence values. Follow the
locking/threading conventions already used by this Red Pitaya application tree.
Do not add a new concurrency framework without need.

After changing excitation frequency, obey the discovered FPGA/DDS settling and
accumulator reset/valid sequence. Never label stale I/Q as belonging to the new
frequency.

## Internal data model

Define typed internal structures rather than passing UART/CSV strings internally.
At minimum, plan for these concepts:

- `RawIqSample`: sequence, sensor/channel, frequency, raw I, raw Q, integration
  or sample count, validity/status, timestamp if available.
- `TrackerConfig`: enabled sensors, frequency/search ranges, points, averaging,
  tracking bandwidth, relock settings, and web update rate.
- `SensorEstimate`: frequency, Q, FWHM, standard error, complex response, and
  validity.
- `TrackingQuality`: normalized residual, template gain, requested shift in Hz,
  frequency SE, loss counter, and tracker state.
- `TelemetrySnapshot`: one coherent publication object for all UI-visible fields.

Preserve compatibility at the conceptual/data level with the existing diagnostic
records, where applicable:

```text
RTB, RTB_INF, RTB_REF, RTB_RES, RTB_MODEL, RTB_PT,
RTM, RTD, RTS, RTQ
```

UART/CSV output may remain as a serialization adapter, but the browser must not
parse these text lines. Expose structured Red Pitaya parameters/signals instead.

The useful `RTQ` fields are:

```text
sequence, id, normalized_residual, template_gain,
requested_shift_hz, se_hz, loss_counter
```

Tracker state should be decided by the backend, for example `STOPPED`,
`SEARCHING`, `TRACKING`, `DEGRADED`, `RELOCKING`, and `ERROR`. JavaScript only
renders it.

## Delivery plan

Work in small, buildable milestones. Do not start by porting the entire tracker.

### Milestone 0 — discovery and design

- Complete `docs/raw_iq_interface.md`.
- Complete a concise `docs/architecture.md` showing threads/control flow,
  ownership, data rates, and the parameter/signal contract.
- Identify exactly which impedance-analyzer files/patterns will be reused and
  which algorithmic parts will not.
- Report uncertainties before implementing around them.

### Milestone 1 — minimal raw-IQ web app (complete)

- Create a buildable `resonance_tracker` app registered consistently with sibling
  apps.
- Implement start/stop and safe initialization/cleanup.
- Read coherent raw I/Q from the existing FPGA interface.
- Show connection state, run state, sequence/update rate, excitation frequency,
  raw I, raw Q, magnitude, and phase.
- Provide a bounded short history plot or diagnostic table.
- No full search/tracking algorithm yet.

Milestone 1 acceptance criteria:

- Builds with the repository's documented app build flow.
- Loads without browser console errors.
- Start/stop can be repeated without hanging or leaking the acquisition resource.
- Frequency changes do not publish stale I/Q under the new frequency.
- UI refresh remains decoupled from measurement timing.
- Invalid/not-ready/overflow conditions are visible rather than converted into
  plausible measurements.

Before starting Milestone 2A, verify these criteria against the implementation and
record any exception. Do not repeat Milestone 1 unless a regression is discovered.

### Milestone 2A — dashboards, baseline, diagnostics, and resonance finding

- Add one authoritative backend instrument state machine.
- Add separate frontend dashboards for stopped, raw-IQ, baseline, diagnostics,
  and tracking while keeping one Red Pitaya application and one transport
  connection.
- Keep backend instrument state separate from the browser's selected diagnostic
  view, as described below.
- Implement the baseline acquisition workflow and its progress/result contract
  using the existing coherent raw-IQ path.
- Implement resonance candidate detection and refinement from the completed
  baseline scan. Publish candidate bounds, score, fitted frequency, Q/FWHM,
  frequency standard error, and model-quality information when available.
- Add a diagnostics acquisition mode similar to the previous `RTM` output. It
  performs or exposes a bounded set of complex measurement points around a
  selected resonance without starting the continuous tracking loop.
- Add a hardware-independent tracker API.
- Add a replay/test source capable of feeding captured frequency/I/Q samples.
- Integrate the existing baseline, candidate search, background removal, and
  resonance fitting logic from the user's implementation where present; do not
  re-invent it from memory.
- Add deterministic replay tests for baseline validity, candidate selection,
  rejected/no-resonance data, and fitted resonance results.

Milestone 2A acceptance criteria:

- Existing raw-IQ acquisition and display still work without regression.
- Backend state transitions are explicit, validated, and published to the UI.
- Changing dashboard tabs never changes hardware state by itself.
- Backend state changes can automatically select a sensible dashboard without
  preventing the user from opening compatible diagnostic views.
- Baseline acquisition reports progress, cancellation, completion, invalid data,
  and error states; it never presents a partial baseline as complete.
- Resonance finding never reports a valid result from an incomplete or mismatched
  baseline sequence.
- Replay tests cover a valid resonance, multiple candidates, and a scan with no
  acceptable resonance.
- Diagnostics points carry one coherent sequence and include sensor/id, signed
  offset, effective frequency, and complex response.
- Hidden dashboards do not perform unnecessary plot rendering or accumulate
  unbounded history.

### Milestone 2B — tracking, quality monitoring, loss, and relocking

- Add the continuous tracking loop using the validated Milestone 2A resonance
  result as its starting point.
- Integrate the existing tracking template/model update logic rather than
  re-implementing it from memory.
- Publish per-update frequency, Q/width where calculated, frequency standard
  error, and the `RTQ` quality fields.
- Implement explicit `TRACKING`, `DEGRADED`, and `RELOCKING` transitions.
- Implement loss detection and bounded relocking so temporary loss does not
  unnecessarily restart the complete baseline workflow.
- Make relock cancellation and stop commands responsive.
- Add deterministic replay tests for stable tracking, commanded shifts, degraded
  quality, loss, successful local relock, and relock failure.

Milestone 2B acceptance criteria:

- Milestone 1 and Milestone 2A remain operational without regression.
- Tracking never starts without a valid selected resonance and compatible
  baseline/model sequence.
- Replay tests exercise normal tracking, degradation, loss, successful relock,
  and failed relock.
- Relocking is bounded by documented frequency/time/attempt limits and exposes
  progress and failure reasons.
- Diagnostics can display the current or last complete RTM-like point set without
  changing tracker state.
- UI and logging rates remain decoupled from the tracking loop.

### Milestone 3 — two-sensor dashboard

- Add sensor 1/2 enable and visibility controls.
- Show frequency, Q, frequency SE, quality, and state per sensor.
- Add trajectory and diagnostic complex-I/Q views.
- Extend the Milestone 2 diagnostics view to compare/select both sensors, then add
  RTD statistics/histograms and RTQ trend analysis after the core data contracts
  are stable.

## UI contract

Prefer the parameter and signal conventions used by current sibling apps. Choose
final names only after inspecting those conventions, then document them in
`docs/architecture.md`.

Initial controls:

- run/stop;
- excitation frequency in Hz;
- integration/averaging setting only if safely supported by the current FPGA API;
- web telemetry rate;
- sensor/channel selector only if the hardware interface already exposes it.

Initial telemetry:

- backend connection and error state;
- acquisition state and monotonic sequence;
- requested and effective frequency;
- raw I and Q integers;
- scaled/normalized I and Q, if scaling is known;
- magnitude and phase with documented calculation;
- valid/busy/overflow flags;
- acquisition and web publication rates.

Do not expose a control that the backend cannot apply and verify. Disable or omit
unsupported controls instead of making them cosmetic.

## Instrument state and dashboard architecture

Use one Red Pitaya application, one backend connection, and several frontend
dashboard modules. Do not create separate Red Pitaya applications for raw-IQ,
baseline, diagnostics, tracking, or stopped modes.

Keep two concepts distinct:

- `INSTRUMENT_STATE`: authoritative backend state describing what the hardware
  and tracker are actually doing.
- `ACTIVE_VIEW`: browser-local selection describing which dashboard the user is
  inspecting.

Changing `ACTIVE_VIEW` must not start, stop, or reconfigure acquisition. Hardware
actions require an explicit command/control handled and acknowledged by the
backend. The UI must always display the real `INSTRUMENT_STATE`, even when the
user is inspecting another dashboard.

Use explicit backend states rather than a collection of loosely related Boolean
flags. Adapt names to established repository conventions, but retain equivalent
semantics:

```text
STOPPED
RAW_IQ
BASELINE_ACQUIRING
BASELINE_READY
RESONANCE_FINDING
DIAGNOSTICS
SEARCHING
TRACKING
DEGRADED
RELOCKING
ERROR
```

Not every backend state needs a separate dashboard. Use this default mapping:

```text
STOPPED             -> stopped dashboard
RAW_IQ              -> raw-IQ dashboard
BASELINE_ACQUIRING  -> baseline dashboard
BASELINE_READY      -> baseline dashboard
RESONANCE_FINDING   -> baseline dashboard
DIAGNOSTICS         -> diagnostics dashboard
SEARCHING           -> tracking dashboard
TRACKING            -> tracking dashboard
DEGRADED            -> tracking dashboard
RELOCKING           -> tracking dashboard
ERROR               -> preserve current view and show a global error banner
```

An important backend transition may automatically select its default dashboard,
but the user may subsequently inspect another compatible view. For example, the
raw-IQ or diagnostics view should remain inspectable while tracking if the
backend publishes the required data. Selecting the diagnostics dashboard during
tracking displays the current or last complete RTM-like point set; it must not
silently change the instrument to `DIAGNOSTICS`. A historical baseline view must
be clearly labelled as the last completed baseline and must not trigger a new
acquisition.

Recommended frontend organization:

```text
js/
  app.js
  state.js
  transport.js
  dashboards/
    stopped.js
    raw_iq.js
    baseline.js
    diagnostics.js
    tracking.js
```

Follow the module/build conventions already used in the checked-out application;
the filenames above are conceptual if the existing frontend does not use ES
modules. Each dashboard should have equivalent lifecycle operations:

```text
enter(latestSnapshot)
update(latestSnapshot)
leave()
```

`leave()` must stop view-specific timers and expensive rendering. Hidden views
may retain bounded data needed for quick inspection, but must not continually
redraw plots. Use one central function to switch visibility and lifecycle state;
do not scatter mode-dependent DOM hiding throughout transport callbacks.

Maintain a shared frontend data store containing the latest coherent backend
snapshot. Transport code updates the store; active dashboard code renders it.
Dashboards must not directly own the WebSocket connection.

Publish a stable superset of small scalar status parameters. Publish large arrays
only when produced or needed, and include sequence/version information so the UI
cannot combine arrays from different acquisitions. Candidate groups include:

```text
APP_STATE, APP_ERROR, APP_SEQUENCE
IQ_SEQUENCE, IQ_FREQUENCY_HZ, IQ_RAW_I, IQ_RAW_Q
BASELINE_SEQUENCE, BASELINE_PROGRESS, BASELINE_VALID
BASELINE_FREQUENCY[], BASELINE_RE[], BASELINE_IM[]
RESONANCE_SEQUENCE, RESONANCE_COUNT, RESONANCE_SELECTED
DIAG_SEQUENCE, DIAG_SENSOR_ID, DIAG_COMPLETE
DIAG_OFFSET[], DIAG_FREQUENCY_HZ[], DIAG_RE[], DIAG_IM[]
TRACK_SEQUENCE, TRACK_F1_HZ, TRACK_F2_HZ
TRACK_Q1, TRACK_Q2, TRACK_STATE_1, TRACK_STATE_2
```

These are conceptual names, not instructions to rename already working Milestone
1 parameters. Reuse compatible existing names and document the final contract.

Backend transition commands must be validated. Reject illegal transitions with a
clear error instead of forcing the state. At minimum, define behavior for:

- stopping from every active state;
- cancelling baseline acquisition;
- starting baseline only when acquisition resources are available;
- starting diagnostics as an explicit acquisition command versus merely opening
  the diagnostics dashboard;
- cancelling diagnostics without invalidating the last complete diagnostic set;
- starting search/tracking only with a valid baseline if the algorithm requires
  one;
- handling acquisition errors and recovery from `ERROR`;
- preventing stale completion callbacks from changing a newer state.

## Numerical and RF requirements

- Preserve raw integer data until a named conversion function.
- Use sufficiently wide intermediate types for accumulator values and magnitude
  calculations; avoid signed overflow and unintended integer truncation.
- Use `hypot`/`atan2` or equivalent for magnitude/phase.
- Define whether phase is degrees or radians and keep it consistent across API,
  logs, and UI.
- Document I/Q scaling. If absolute scaling is unknown, call the values
  normalized or ADC/accumulator units rather than volts.
- Track requested versus realizable DDS/NCO frequency separately when tuning is
  quantized.
- Any averaging must state whether it averages raw accumulations, complex I/Q,
  magnitude, or fitted estimates. Prefer coherent complex averaging where valid.
- Represent invalid data explicitly; do not use zero as an implicit error value.

## Coding and repository rules

- Match the language standard, formatting, naming, and build conventions already
  used by the nearest maintained Red Pitaya web apps.
- Reuse repository APIs and utilities before introducing dependencies.
- Keep changes scoped to the new app and the smallest necessary build/registration
  files.
- Never overwrite or discard unrelated working-tree changes.
- Do not edit vendored/generated files unless that is the established build path.
- Avoid large mechanical formatting changes in borrowed code.
- Add comments for hardware sequencing, units, ownership, and non-obvious timing;
  do not narrate straightforward code.
- Keep configuration centralized. Do not scatter 125 MHz, 30 MHz, register masks,
  or update intervals as magic numbers.
- Do not silently copy code with incompatible licensing. Retain required headers.

## Validation

For every milestone:

1. Run the narrowest relevant unit tests and app build.
2. Run formatting/static checks used by this repository for modified files.
3. If cross-compilation or target hardware is unavailable, state exactly what was
   and was not validated; do not claim runtime verification.
4. When hardware is available, capture a short test showing monotonically updated
   I/Q at a fixed frequency, then a controlled frequency change with correct
   reset/settling behavior.
5. Check browser console and backend logs for errors.
6. Summarize modified files, data-contract decisions, tests, and remaining
   hardware questions.

Do not weaken or delete an existing test simply to make a change pass.

## Definition of done for the next Codex task

The next Codex task is complete when Milestone 2A is implemented and validated as
far as the available environment permits. Milestone 1 must remain operational.
It is not complete if dashboard selection directly controls hardware, if
baseline or diagnostics arrays can be published partially as valid, if resonance
finding accepts stale/mismatched scan data, or if tests exercise only the happy
path. Continuous tracking and relocking belong to Milestone 2B and should not be
mixed into the Milestone 2A change unless required by an already shared core API.

Before proceeding to Milestone 2B, present the user with:

- the implemented backend state-transition table;
- the final parameter/signal and dashboard contracts;
- baseline acquisition timing and validity behavior;
- resonance-candidate and fit results for representative replay data;
- the diagnostics/RTM-like point contract and visualization behavior;
- replay-test results for valid, ambiguous, and missing resonances;
- raw-IQ regression-test results and achieved acquisition/UI rates;
- target-hardware checks still required;
- any decision still needed for two-sensor multiplexing or simultaneous
  acquisition.

Milestone 2B is complete when its tracking, quality, loss, and relock acceptance
criteria pass. Before proceeding to Milestone 3, present the tracking/relock
replay results, achieved loop rates, relock bounds, failure behavior, and any
remaining target-hardware validation.
